	"web_text": "Brisé fans imitating Japanese court fans (hiogi) were introduced to Europe via China in the late sixteenth century. Hiogi were folding fans made of thin strips of wood, usually cypress, which were pierced and held together by a rivet at one end. Ivory brisé fans were common in the first quarter of the eighteenth century, with their smooth sticks allowing for vivid painted designs. This fan is lavishly ornamented with painted floral and geometric motifs, several small portraits, and a gripping scene at center, which shows the abduction of Helen of Troy.",
	"didactic_label": "Brisé fans imitating Japanese court fans (hiogi) were introduced to Europe via China in the late sixteenth century. Hiogi were folding fans made of thin strips of wood, usually cypress, which were pierced and held together by a rivet at one end. Ivory brisé fans were common in the first quarter of the eighteenth century, with their smooth sticks allowing for vivid painted designs. This fan is lavishly ornamented with painted floral and geometric motifs, several small portraits, and a gripping scene at center, which shows the abduction of Helen of Troy.",
